  "title": "Purr 2FA Authenticator",
  "shortDescription": "Your 2FA codes stay on your phone. Sign in on your computer with one tap.",
  "fullDescription": "A password on its own is not enough. Purr is the second factor, the part a thief on the other side of the world cannot reach, because it lives in your pocket.\n\nPurr keeps your two-factor codes where they belong: on your phone, and nowhere else. When a website asks for a code, only the six digits ever travel. Your secrets never leave the device.\n\nWHY PURR\n\nOne tap on your computer. Pair the browser extension once, and signing in on your laptop becomes a single tap on your phone. No typing, no hunting for the right account.\n\nNothing leaves this phone but six digits. Your codes are generated on the device and stay there. Purr works on a plane, with no signal, anywhere.\n\nNo account, no email. There is nothing to sign up for and no address to hand over. You just set it up and go.\n\nA way back if you lose your phone. Print a twelve-word recovery kit once and you can restore every account onto a new device, even if you lose everything.\n\nYour codes are yours. Move them to another app any time. There is no lock-in.\n\nOpen source. The whole project is public and auditable under GPL 3.0.\n\nHOW IT WORKS\n\nAdd an account by scanning its setup QR code or typing the key by hand. Purr generates standard time-based one-time passwords (TOTP), so it works with the thousands of sites that support authenticator apps.\n\nTo sign in on a computer, install the Purr browser extension and pair it with your phone by scanning one QR code. From then on, a sign-in request appears on your phone; you approve it, and the code fills itself in. You can also use a hardware security key to fill the code directly in the browser.\n\nPRIVACY\n\nPurr collects no personal information. It has no ads and no trackers. When your phone and computer talk to each other, the connection is end to end encrypted, and the relay that passes messages between them only ever sees scrambled data it cannot read.",
  "extensionSummary": "Sign in on your computer with one tap. Purr brings your 2FA code from your phone or a security key, and never sees your secret.",
  "extensionDescription": "Purr turns signing in on your computer into a single tap.\n\nPair the extension with the Purr app on your phone once, by scanning one QR code. After that, when a website asks for a two-factor code, a request appears on your phone. You approve it, and the six digits fill themselves into the page.\n\nYour secrets never leave your phone. Only the finished six-digit code travels, and only after you approve it. The extension never sees the secret that generates your codes.\n\nWHAT YOU GET\n\nOne tap to sign in. No typing codes, no switching apps, no hunting for the right account.\n\nOnly six digits ever reach the page. The extension fills the code into the field that needs it and nothing else. It does not read or send the contents of the pages you visit.\n\nAn optional security-key route. Touch a registered hardware key (such as a YubiKey) to fill a code directly in the browser, with your phone still in your pocket. Your codes are cached locally only as ciphertext that the key unlocks.\n\nNo account required. Nothing to sign up for.\n\nOpen source, auditable under GPL 3.0.\n\nPRIVACY\n\nThe connection between your phone and this browser is end to end encrypted. The relay that carries messages between them only ever sees scrambled data it cannot read. Purr collects no personal information, shows no ads, and includes no trackers.\n\nYou need the Purr app on your Android phone to use this extension."
